// src/v4/std/cardinality.dag
//
// Scope: cardinality refinement substrate — bounded/unbounded distinctions, no escape.
// Anchor: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cardinality
//
// Owns:
//   - Cardinality refinement types
//   - bounded-iteration discipline (P4 decidability)
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag: Cardinality connective
//
// Status: T-3 modeled 2026-05-16 (stern-hawk-619); descent-evidence
//   carriers + Multiplicity refinement


module v4.std.cardinality

import v4.std.node { Symbol }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// SCOPE. This file serves INVARIANTS P4 (Decidability) in two related
// ways, both assigned by std/node.dag's A2 + Q2 dispositions:
//   - the DESCENT-EVIDENCE carriers — `DescentEvidence`, `RankingDimension`,
//     `TerminationProof` — the structural proof that an EXPLICIT
//     non-structural recursion is well-founded;
//   - the MULTIPLICITY refinement — `Multiplicity` — the type-level
//     bounded/unbounded distinction, with no escape hatch (P4).
//
// node.dag ONLY. The sole import is `Symbol` (opaque kernel identity,
// node.dag K-1). node.dag's header frames `Symbol` as substrate-ambient,
// but the v2 bootstrap seed resolves it only as a node.dag export, so it
// is imported explicitly — node.dag is this file's permitted substrate,
// so consuming it is in scope. `List` is genuinely kernel-ambient (no
// import). "Consumes std/node.dag: the Cardinality connective" (header)
// is the additional CONCEPTUAL grounding — `Multiplicity` refines the
// meaning of node.dag's `Cardinality` connective.
//
// CHECKER, NOT DISCOVERER (INVARIANTS:66 / node.dag A2 / feedback_no_
// engine). A `TerminationProof` is a structural CLAIM of well-founded
// descent. A later pipeline stage — the termination checker — VERIFIES
// that claim against the loop's actual structure; it never SEARCHES for
// a measure. The checker itself is not modeled here (it is a pipeline-
// stage task, T-6+); `DescentEvidence` is the per-edge observation
// vocabulary it emits while verifying — `DescentUnknown` its fail-closed
// value, never a fabricated `Strict`.
//
// THE IMPLICIT CASE HAS NO CARRIER (node.dag A2 / Q2). When a recursion
// descends on a proper sub-Node child, termination is already free from
// node.dag's `node_well_formed` whole-tree recursion — no measure, no
// `RankingDimension`, no proof. The carriers below exist SOLELY for the
// EXPLICIT non-structural case: recursion whose descent is not a direct
// child edge and therefore must name what descends.
//
// NO LATTICE / NO MERGE HERE. `DescentEvidence` is a plain closed sum.
// The conservative meet / optimistic join that merges branch-evidence
// (DescentUnknown ⊑ NonIncreasing ⊑ Strict) has exactly ONE consumer —
// the termination checker that folds evidence across a `Branch`'s arms —
// and that consumer is a later pipeline stage. Modeling a
// `BoundedLattice<DescentEvidence>` inhabitance now would be a carrier
// bolted on ahead of its consumer; it lands in THIS file, one visible
// edit, when the checker exists (cost-of-change = 1).
//
// LEXICOGRAPHIC (Dershowitz-Manna 1979). `TerminationProof` is a
// lexicographic descent certificate: a (possibly empty) priority-ordered
// list of higher-priority `non_increasing` dimensions plus exactly one
// mandatory `strict` dimension. The per-dimension descent fact is encoded
// by STRUCTURAL POSITION — being in `non_increasing` means non-increasing,
// being `strict` means strictly-decreasing — never a stored
// `DescentEvidence` field (a stored field would permit a proof whose
// every entry is non-strict / unknown, i.e. no real witness — the exact
// hole the operator flagged BLOCKING; see `TerminationProof`).
// The multiset-ordering INTERPRETATION (a second Dershowitz-Manna
// extension) is a fold the termination checker performs; it has no T-3
// consumer and is deliberately not a carrier here — a faithful `Multiset`
// would need an unordered collection the substrate does not have (a
// `List` "read as unordered" is non-canonical — CULTURE §8). It lands
// with the checker that needs it (cost-of-change = 1).
//
// LAWS ARE GROUNDED DEFINITIONS, NEVER FLAGS (feedback_no_annotations).
// "Well-founded", "strictly decreases" are the cited definitions of the
// named carriers, not stored booleans.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// DescentEvidence — at one recursive-call edge, the observed relation of
// a ranking measure between caller and callee: it strictly decreases,
// does not increase, or is not determined.
//
// This is the termination CHECKER's per-edge observation vocabulary — the
// result of comparing a measure across a call edge. It is deliberately
// NOT stored as a field of `TerminationProof`: a proof that stored a free
// `DescentEvidence` per dimension could carry an all-non-strict / all-
// `DescentUnknown` value set — a "proof" with no actual witness (the
// operator BLOCKING finding on PR #3163). The proof instead encodes its
// descent claim by STRUCTURAL POSITION (see `TerminationProof`);
// `DescentEvidence` is what the checker EMITS while verifying that claim,
// fail-closed. It is a carrier modeled ahead of its T-6+ consumer, per
// the brief's settled decision (plain closed sum, no lattice).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger —
// five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: a per-edge descent observation is consumed
//      uniformly (the termination checker dispatches on all three to fold
//      a proof); it is not a scattered per-consumer fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: promoting to `{ rank: Int }` (2/1/0)
//      replaces a closed 3-valued exhaustive sum with an unbounded Int
//      (`rank = 42` representable) — violates P2 + the closed-set rule
//      (a closed outcome set is an enum, not a numeric proxy). An
//      `evidence_rank -> Int` PROJECTION is admissible (a derived total
//      order over the three), but it is not the carrier.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: Strict / NonIncreasing / DescentUnknown do
//      not trace to distinct std/ algebraic structures; they ARE the
//      exhaustive trichotomy of comparing a measure across a call edge
//      under a well-founded relation. No richer std/ source.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: one call edge yields EXACTLY ONE of the three
//      — mutually exclusive alternatives, never simultaneous coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: Strict / NonIncreasing /
//      DescentUnknown are the fixed, irreducible per-edge descent-
//      trichotomy outcomes (the codomain of a well-founded relation),
//      not N mechanically-repeated `F<X>` variants enumerating a
//      separately-declared set — there is no source set to project over
//      (Practice 7). No generic / projection collapses them.
// Terminal: the irreducible per-edge descent trichotomy. `DescentUnknown`
// is the fail-closed bottom — the conservative default a checker assigns
// when descent cannot be certified (INVARIANTS P3; never a fabricated
// `Strict`). External authority: well-founded relations (Zermelo 1904,
// von Neumann 1929), ranking functions (Floyd 1967, Turing 1949).
type DescentEvidence
  = Strict
  | NonIncreasing
  | DescentUnknown


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// RankingDimension — one component of a ranking: the measured quantity
// whose well-founded descent ranks an EXPLICIT recursion.
//
// `measured` is the opaque `Symbol` identity of the recursion parameter
// the measure projects (node.dag K-1). This is the v4 dissolution of v3
// `termination.dag`'s `param: String` scaffold: an identifier is now a
// structural opaque identity, not a readable string — a readable string
// is a heuristic-smuggling channel (node.dag K-1, feedback_opaque_
// strings_attract_heuristics). The well-founded ORDER under which the
// measure descends is NOT stored: it is derived from `measured`'s type by
// the checker — storing it would duplicate that authority (P2), and it is
// exactly why v3's `TreeSize | ListLength | ArithmeticValue | …` kind
// enum dissolves (each kind was a proxy for the measured value's type).
type RankingDimension {
  measured: Symbol
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// TerminationProof — a lexicographic descent certificate (Dershowitz-
// Manna 1979) for an EXPLICIT non-structural recursion: the structural
// CLAIM a termination checker VERIFIES (never discovers, INVARIANTS:66).
//
// Shape — and why it is this shape (operator BLOCKING review, PR #3163):
//   - `strict` — the one dimension claimed to strictly decrease. A
//     MANDATORY field, so every `TerminationProof` structurally contains
//     a real descent witness. There is no representable proof with zero
//     dimensions, and none whose descent claim is entirely non-strict —
//     a "proof" that is either is the ABSENCE of a proof (INVARIANTS P2:
//     illegal states unrepresentable).
//   - `non_increasing` — the strictly-higher-priority dimensions, in
//     priority order, each claimed not to increase. Possibly empty (then
//     the certificate is plain single-measure strict descent). Lower-
//     priority dimensions are irrelevant to a lexicographic argument once
//     a strict position is fixed, so they are deliberately not carried.
//
// The per-dimension descent fact is its STRUCTURAL POSITION, never a
// stored value. This is why `DescentEvidence` is NOT a field here: a
// stored `evidence: DescentEvidence` would re-admit a proof whose every
// entry is non-strict / `DescentUnknown` — the convention-level (not
// structural) witness the BLOCKING review rejected. Position-encoding
// makes the witness structural: `Multiplicity::Unbounded`'s `step` now
// carries a real strict-descent witness by construction.
//
// Whether the `strict` claim is TRUE — whether that named dimension
// genuinely decreases in the loop — is unavoidably the checker's
// verification against loop structure (T-6+); the type cannot inspect a
// loop it is not given. The type guarantees a witness EXISTS and has the
// right shape; the checker confirms it HOLDS.
//
// Single-recursive-call scope: this certifies the descent at THE
// recursive call (the brief's single self-recursive function shape). A
// loop with recursive calls in multiple `Branch` arms generalises to a
// per-edge collection — that lands when the `Loop` / `Branch` edge
// structure is modeled (T-6+, node.dag's "NAMED OBLIGATION ON T-6+");
// cost-of-change = 1, no T-3 consumer needs it yet.
type TerminationProof {
  non_increasing: List<RankingDimension>
  strict: RankingDimension
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Multiplicity — the cardinality refinement: is a multiplicity's extent
// bounded (finite) or unbounded (infinite)? This refines the meaning of
// node.dag's `Cardinality` connective for P4 decidability.
// Anchor: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cardinality (finite vs infinite).
//
// THE ASYMMETRY IS THE NO-ESCAPE-HATCH. `Bounded` is the fail-closed-safe
// default and carries nothing — asserting finite extent is the ordinary,
// decidable case. `Unbounded` CANNOT be constructed without a
// `TerminationProof`: genuine unboundedness (servers / streams / REPLs)
// is, per node.dag A2, "a TERMINATING step iterated by the coordination-
// boundary driver — never an unbounded `.dag` loop". `step` is that
// witness — the proof the per-iteration step terminates. An unbounded-
// without-witness state is therefore structurally unrepresentable
// (INVARIANTS P4): there is no way to name something unbounded without
// exhibiting that its step is itself bounded. The witness cannot be
// vacuous OR non-strict either — `TerminationProof` carries a mandatory
// `strict` ranking dimension whose descent fact is its structural
// position, so every `Unbounded`'s `step` contains a real strict-descent
// witness by construction. The escape hatch is closed at the type level,
// not by convention (operator BLOCKING review, PR #3163).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger —
// five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: a multiplicity refinement is consumed
//      uniformly by the P4 decidability check; not a scattered per-
//      consumer fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the variants have DIFFERENT shapes
//      (`Bounded` nullary; `Unbounded { step }`). Collapsing to
//      `{ unbounded: Bool, step?: TerminationProof }` makes "bounded
//      carrying a step" and "unbounded with no step" representable — the
//      precise inverse of the no-escape-hatch requirement (P2: illegal
//      states unrepresentable).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: `Bounded` / `Unbounded` do not trace to
//      distinct std/ algebraic structures; they ARE the two alternatives
//      of finite-vs-infinite extent (cardinality theory).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: an extent is EITHER bounded OR unbounded —
//      genuine, mutually-exclusive alternatives (no value is both), not
//      simultaneous coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: `Bounded` and `Unbounded` are not a
//      mechanical `F<X>` shape repeated over a separately-declared set
//      (they have different arities — nullary vs. payload-carrying);
//      there is no generic / projection (Practice 7) that replaces the
//      two-way finite/infinite distinction.
// Terminal: the irreducible finite/infinite extent dichotomy, grounded in
// cardinality theory. (INVARIANTS P1 names `BoundDeclaration =
// ExactBound | AnyBound | … ` as a correct alternatives-sum of the same
// family.)
type Multiplicity
  = Bounded
  | Unbounded { step: TerminationProof }

